Fixes #3953
Fixes #3848
Fixes #3849
Fixes #3850

## Problem and changes

Combines four related `queues` analysis and check improvements on top of the shared queue graph and job-option resolution pipeline:

### 1. Workspace `exports` wildcards for worker queue-name constants and deterministic fixed-queue anchors (#3953)

Queue resolvers previously failed to resolve workspace package `exports` wildcards (such as `@acme/queues/*`) when tracing `new Worker(QUEUE_NAME, ...)` and `new Queue(QUEUE_NAME, ...)` constants, causing workers to appear unmatched, and undeclared fixed-`queueName` factory wrappers anchored on the lexicographically first caller file. This change wraps queue resolvers in `WorkspaceFallbackResolver`, records `queue_name_import` for `new Worker` and `LocalQueue::ImportedName` for `new Queue`, anchors undeclared fixed-`queueName` wrappers on a deterministic virtual `queue:<name>`, and documents wildcard support in `docs/configuration/queues.md`.

### 2. Opt-in `unbounded-job-retention` check (#3848)

BullMQ / GlideMQ queues without a retention bound on `removeOnComplete` / `removeOnFail` accumulate completed or failed jobs in Redis indefinitely. This change adds opt-in `queues.unboundedJobRetention` (`boolean | { require?: ("removeOnComplete" | "removeOnFail")[], allowPaths?: string[] }`) in config v2 (default disabled), reusing the shared job-option resolution pass across `defaultJobOptions`, direct `add`/`addBulk`/`upsertJobScheduler` calls, `FlowProducer`, and enqueue wrappers.

In `job_options_retention_read.rs`, `classify_value` treats `true`, every numeric literal (including `0`, which removes jobs immediately), and keep-objects specifying a non-null/non-undefined `count` or `age` as bounded; it flags explicit `false`, keep-objects without `count`/`age` (`{}`), and omitted retention keys when neither call-site options nor `defaultJobOptions` supply a bound. Same-file `const` bindings are resolved, and non-literal or open-spread values abstain. When `unboundedJobRetention` covers `removeOnComplete` or `removeOnFail` on a non-allowlisted file, `unbounded-job-retention` reports the retention key and `requiredJobOptions` (`missing-job-option`) skips those covered keys so the same site does not emit duplicate findings.

### 3. Opt-in advisory `job-state-as-record` check (#3849)

Using Redis job state as a durable record of work leads to lost retries once retention trims old jobs. This change adds opt-in `queues.jobStateAsRecord` (`boolean | { allowPaths?: string[] }`) in config v2 (default disabled) along with `CheckSeverity` (`"error" | "advisory"`) and optional `severity` on `CheckFinding`. Advisory-only findings exit `0` in standalone `queues check` and demote into `advisories` (after suppression) in aggregate `check`.

The check flags two function-scoped patterns only when the queue receiver (and any `.add`/`.addBulk` target) resolves to a configured queue: `queue.getJob(...)` followed by `.getState()` / `.isFailed()` / `.isCompleted()` guarding a queue mutation (`.retry()`, `.remove()`, `.add()`, `.addBulk()`), and `queue.getFailed()` / `queue.getCompleted()` / `queue.getJobs(...)` feeding `.add()`, `.addBulk()`, or `.retry()`. It abstains on unresolved receivers (such as `Set.add(...)` or plain objects), read-only status inspection, and `allowPaths` matches.

### 4. `required-job-options` follow-ups (#3850)

Abstains on unresolved `addBulk` and `upsertJobScheduler` receivers (matching `add`), tracks local construction declaration spans, marks ambiguous multi-definition queues instead of picking the first definition file in sorted order, and keeps `crates/no-mistakes/src/queue/graph.rs` within the 200 physical-line limit (194 lines).

